.TH "KRES" 1 "2026-09-29" "toolbelt 0.1.0" "toolbelt manual"
.SH NAME
kres \- compare node requests with real usage
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
kres [--sort cpu|mem] [-- kubectl options]
kres --pods NODE [--sort cpu|mem] [-- kubectl options]
.fi
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The scheduler places a pod by what it asks for, its requests, and not by what it uses. A node whose pods ask for 90% of its cpu takes no more pods that ask for much, even when that node sits almost idle. That is the usual answer to a pod that stays Pending with \fBInsufficient cpu\fR on a cluster that looks quiet. The other way round hurts too. A pod that uses far more than it asks for crowds its neighbours, and under memory pressure the kubelet evicts it first.
.PP
\fBkubectl top nodes\fR shows usage and \fBkubectl describe node\fR shows requests, one node at a time. \fBkres\fR puts both on one row per node, as amounts and as a share of what the node can give, its allocatable cpu and memory. Below the table it flags every node with 90% or more of its cpu or memory requested, and says how big a new pod can be and still fit there.
.PP
\fBkres \-\-pods NODE\fR breaks one node down by pod, with each pod's requests and usage, and flags pods that use more than twice what they ask for.
.PP
\fBkres\fR only reads. It never changes requests, limits or pods.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-pods NODE\fR
List the pods on NODE with their requests and usage.
.TP
\fB\-\-sort cpu\fR or \fB\-\-sort mem\fR
Sort rows by requests of that kind, highest first. Node order, or pod order, by default.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Print only the table, with no header line, flags or notes.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Print each kubectl command before it runs.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show the help.
.SH HOW IT COUNTS
.PP
Allocatable : What the node offers to pods, from the node's \fBstatus.allocatable\fR. It is the node's size minus what the kubelet keeps for the system. Percentages are shares of this.
.PP
Requests : The sum over the pods on the node that still hold their requests, so Succeeded and Failed pods do not count. A pod counts as the larger of its containers together and its biggest init container, plus its \fBoverhead\fR. That is the same sum the scheduler uses. Containers with no request count as 0.
.PP
Used : What metrics\-server measured, from \fBkubectl top nodes\fR or \fBkubectl top pods\fR. It is a recent sample, not a peak.
.PP
Units : cpu prints in millicores under one core, such as \fB450m\fR, and in cores above, such as \fB1.8\fR. Memory prints in \fBMi\fR under one GiB and in \fBGi\fR above.
.SH FLAGS
.PP
\fB! NODE has N% of its cpu requested, new pods asking for more than X will stay Pending there\fR : Printed for each node at 90% or more of its cpu or memory requested. X is what is left.
.PP
\fB! POD uses X against a Y memory request\fR, and the same for cpu : Printed in the \fB\-\-pods\fR view for each pod using more than twice its request. Raise its request, or find out why it grew.
.PP
The flags are advice. \fBkres\fR exits 0 with or without them.
.SH PASS\-THROUGH
.PP
Options after \fB\-\-\fR go to every kubectl call.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ kres -- --context kubernetes-admin@kubernetes
.fi
.RE
.SH NEEDS
.PP
\fBkubectl\fR and \fBjq\fR. The usage columns need metrics\-server in the cluster. Without it, \fBkres\fR still prints requests and shows \fB\-\fR for usage. On kind, install metrics\-server with its release manifest and add the argument \fB\-\-kubelet\-insecure\-tls\fR to its container, since kind's kubelets use self\-signed certificates.
.PP
The account behind your context needs \fBlist\fR on nodes and on pods in every namespace, and \fBget\fR on the metrics API for the usage.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Every node
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ kres
context kind-kind, 3 nodes
              CPU                   MEMORY
NODE          requests   used       requests    used
k8s-master    450m 23%   410m 21%   170Mi 4%    2.3Gi 61%
k8s-worker-1  1.8 90%    620m 31%   3.0Gi 79%   2.1Gi 54%
k8s-worker-2  300m 15%   1.4 70%    768Mi 20%   3.4Gi 90%
! k8s-worker-1 has 90% of its cpu requested, new pods
  asking for more than 200m will stay Pending there
.fi
.RE
.PP
k8s\-worker\-1 uses 31% of its cpu and still has room for only 200m of new requests. k8s\-worker\-2 is the other way round, with low requests and high real usage.
.SS Sorted by memory requests
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ kres --sort mem
context kind-kind, 3 nodes
              CPU                   MEMORY
NODE          requests   used       requests    used
k8s-worker-1  1.8 90%    620m 31%   3.0Gi 79%   2.1Gi 54%
k8s-worker-2  300m 15%   1.4 70%    768Mi 20%   3.4Gi 90%
k8s-master    450m 23%   410m 21%   170Mi 4%    2.3Gi 61%
! k8s-worker-1 has 90% of its cpu requested, new pods
  asking for more than 200m will stay Pending there
.fi
.RE
.SS The pods on one node
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ kres --pods k8s-worker-2
context kind-kind, node k8s-worker-2, 2 pods
POD                      CPU req  used   MEM req  used
monitoring/prometheus-0  200m     610m   512Mi    1.9Gi
monitoring/loki-0        100m     240m   256Mi    820Mi
! prometheus-0 uses 1.9Gi against a 512Mi memory request
! prometheus-0 uses 610m cpu against a 200m request
! loki-0 uses 820Mi against a 256Mi memory request
! loki-0 uses 240m cpu against a 100m request
.fi
.RE
.PP
Both pods ask for a fraction of what they use. That is why the node is at 90% memory while its requests are at 20%, and why it is the first to evict.
.SS No request, no usage yet
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ kres --pods k8s-master --sort mem
context kind-kind, node k8s-master, 3 pods
POD                                    CPU req  used   MEM req  used
kube-system/etcd-k8s-master            100m     -      100Mi    -
kube-system/coredns-1                  100m     -      70Mi     -
kube-system/kube-apiserver-k8s-master  250m     -      -        -
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fB\-\fR under a request means the pod asks for none. \fB\-\fR under used means metrics\-server has no sample for the pod, which is normal for a minute after it starts.
.SS No metrics\-server
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ kres
context kind-kind, 3 nodes
              CPU                   MEMORY
NODE          requests   used       requests    used
k8s-master    450m 23%   -          170Mi 4%    -
k8s-worker-1  1.8 90%    -          3.0Gi 79%   -
k8s-worker-2  300m 15%   -          768Mi 20%   -
! k8s-worker-1 has 90% of its cpu requested, new pods
  asking for more than 200m will stay Pending there
kres: no metrics API, usage columns are empty.
kres: install metrics-server to fill them.
.fi
.RE
.SS Only the table
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ kres -q
              CPU                   MEMORY
NODE          requests   used       requests    used
k8s-master    450m 23%   410m 21%   170Mi 4%    2.3Gi 61%
k8s-worker-1  1.8 90%    620m 31%   3.0Gi 79%   2.1Gi 54%
k8s-worker-2  300m 15%   1.4 70%    768Mi 20%   3.4Gi 90%
.fi
.RE
.SS What it runs
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ kres -v > /dev/null
+ kubectl get nodes -o json
+ kubectl get pods -A --field-selector 'status.phase!=Succeeded,status.phase!=Failed' -o json
+ kubectl top nodes --no-headers
.fi
.RE
.SS A node that does not exist
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ kres --pods nope
kres: no node nope. Nodes are k8s-master, k8s-worker-1 and k8s-worker-2
.fi
.RE
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.PP
The usage columns show \fB\-\fR although metrics\-server runs : metrics\-server needs a minute after it starts, and it fails quietly when it cannot reach the kubelets. Check \fBkubectl \-n kube\-system logs deploy/metrics\-server\fR for certificate errors.
.PP
A pod stays Pending, and every node shows room : \fBkres\fR sums cpu and memory only. A pod can also wait for a node with a label it selects, a taint it tolerates, a free host port, or a volume in its zone. \fBkwhy\fR shows the scheduler's own message.
.PP
The totals are lower than \fBkubectl describe node\fR on a node with sidecars : Sidecar containers declared as init containers with \fBrestartPolicy: Always\fR run next to the app for the pod's whole life, so the scheduler adds them to the app's request. \fBkres\fR counts them as plain init containers, so a pod with sidecars shows a little less than the scheduler reserves.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
It printed the table, with or without flags.
.TP
1
No such node, or kubectl failed.
.TP
2
Bad usage, such as \fB\-\-sort\fR with another word.
.TP
3
kubectl or jq is missing.
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
\fBknodes\fR, \fBkwhy\fR, \fBmem\fR, \fBkubectl\-top(1)\fR
